{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310565,"nonce":"B764/glSDUq6+ASPaUm45GpEEz4=","title":"MEASUREMENT TRAP — haxe-conformance/run.sh defaults LLVM=0, so the scored path is the DEFAULT TIERED path (interpreter -\u003e Beadie -\u003e ...","message":"`LLVM=\"${LLVM:-0}\"` in `haxe-conformance/run.sh`. The scored invocation is\n`rayzor run --release --no-cache --preset application` (lowercase preset -- `Application` is\nrejected by clap), run from the case dir under `${TMPDIR}/rayzor_conformance/run/\u003cCase\u003e`.\n\n**The trap:** Issue8220 was logged CRASH `uncompiled unit.issues.Issue8220.main` by two consecutive\nbatteries, yet passed 8/8 when re-run by hand with `--llvm` -- including 16/16 under 8-way\nconcurrency, which is what made it look like harness flake. It is not flake: `--llvm` compiles the\ngeneric template that Cranelift refuses, so the LLVM run has no stub at all. Drop `--llvm` and it\nreproduces 3/3.\n\nThe two backends also fail DIFFERENTLY, so the message tells you which path you are on:\n- Cranelift: `[trap-stub:generic]` / `[trap-stub:cascade]` (needs `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1`).\n- LLVM: `rayzor: \\`X\\` was never compiled` from `is_forward_stub()` at llvm_jit_backend.rs (a\n  BODYLESS function), a different defect class entirely.\n\n**Issue6480 is BISTABLE on one binary** -- six runs of the same build gave\n`OK FAIL OK FAIL OK OK`. A +-1 swing in the battery total that lands on that row\nis the flake, not a regression; re-run it half a dozen times before attributing\nit to a change. Issue4675 and Issue3635 have behaved the same way.\n\n**2026-09-20, bit me the other way:** a recreated gate script ran `LLVM=1 run.sh`\nand reported 577 -\u003e 535 with ~40 LOST rows (`%` on static Ints = 255 under\n`--llvm`, MacroStringTools import failures). None of it was the diff; the\ndefault-tier rerun of the same binary was clean. A gate script must run\n`run.sh` with NO tier flag (CI does), and the base and new reports must come\nfrom the same flags. Also: a binary COPIED out of a worktree loses its\nexe-relative haxe-std — run worktree builds in place.\n\nBefore diagnosing any conformance row, reproduce it with the harness's own flags.\nSee [[project_haxe_conformance_baseline.md]], [[feedback_perf_measurement_protocol.md]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_conformance_scores_the_tiered_path_not_llvm` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}